### Did you read the migration guide?

- [x] I have read the whole migration guide

### Is there an existing issue that is already proposing this?

- [x] I have searched the existing issues

### Potential Commit/PR that introduced the regression

PR #17563 (fix for #17562), released in v11.2.3

### NestJS version

11.2.2 -> 11.2.3 (still present in 11.2.7 and 12.1.3)

### Describe the regression

Consider 2 request-scoped providers that depend on each other, but only one of them uses `forwardRef`. The `CircularDependencyException` message implies each side must use `forwardRef`, so this isn't supported. Nest already rejects this, but only in a specific resolve order:

- Resolving `CreateUsecase` first, then `UpdateUsecase` after (each in its own request) results in the first `resolve()` being rejected with `CircularDependencyException` (since v11.1.20).
- Resolving `UpdateUsecase` first, then `CreateUsecase` after (since v11.2.3) results in the second `resolve()` hanging and never settling without an error or log message.

Note that, up to v11.2.2, the second `resolve()` returned with `update.create` as `undefined`. It was returning a half-built instance, but now it just hangs.

It seems that the hang is caused by the `else` branch that PR #17563 added to `Injector.resolveComponentHost` (see [v12.1.3 source](https://github.com/nestjs/nest/blob/v12.1.3/packages/core/injector/injector.ts#L644-L657)). The comment there states:

> if a circular dependency is truly in-flight, the nested lookup will find this host pending and defer through its `donePromise`.

In this case, however, the call never settles and the cycle is neither deferred nor detected.

Some similar earlier cases are #17870 / #17871, where a cycle that hung was turned into a `CircularDependencyException`.

### Minimum reproduction code

https://github.com/joaocarmo/nest-request-scope-forwardref-hang

### Input code

```ts
// update.usecase.ts
import { Inject, Injectable, Scope, forwardRef } from "@nestjs/common";
import { CreateUsecase } from "./create.usecase";

@Injectable({ scope: Scope.REQUEST })
export class UpdateUsecase {
  constructor(
    @Inject(forwardRef(() => CreateUsecase))
    public readonly create: CreateUsecase,
  ) {}
}
```

```ts
// create.usecase.ts
import { Injectable, Scope } from "@nestjs/common";
import { UpdateUsecase } from "./update.usecase";

@Injectable({ scope: Scope.REQUEST })
export class CreateUsecase {
  constructor(public readonly update: UpdateUsecase) {}
}
```

### Expected behavior

`resolve()` rejects with `CircularDependencyException` in both scenarios, since it already does that when `CreateUsecase` is resolved first.

### Other

- Node.js v24.21.0 on a macOS
- With `forwardRef` on both sides, both v11.2.3 and v12.1.3 resolve correctly in both scenarios. This issue is only about the apparently missing error in the one-sided case.

