Node.js version: 24.21.0 (as bundled in Electron 43.7.3 / VS Code 1.140.0)
Platform: macOS arm64 (likely affects all platforms)

## Description

When the V8 inspector is attached and an `IncomingMessage` response has had `setEncoding()` called on it, the inspector's data listener in `lib/internal/inspector/network_http.js` throws `TypeError: Missing dataLength` in event. This throw propagates through `EventEmitter.emit` uncaug and causes Node's stream  machinery to call `destroy(err)` on the socket. The caller receives `reason: aborted` with no indication of the real cause.

## Root cause

`network_http.js` passes `chunk.byteLength` as `dataLength` to the C++ `NetworkAgent::dataReceived` handler. When `response.setEncoding('utf8')` has been called, Node emits string chunks instead of Buffer
chunks — strings have `.length` but not `.byteLength`, so `chunk.byteLength` is `undefined`. The C++ `ObjectGetInt` validation fails and throws.

## Suggested fix

```js
// lib/internal/inspector/network_http.js
dataLength: typeof chunk === 'string' ? Buffer.byteLength(chunk) : chunk.byteLength,
```

## Reproduction                                                                                                                                                                                      
Attach the V8 inspector (e.g. VS Code F5 debugger) to a Node process that makes an `https.request` call and calls `response.setEncoding('utf8')` in the response handler. The request will abort with `reason: aborted` instead of completing successfully. The same code works without the debugger attached.

### Example
```js
const data = [];
response.setEncoding('utf8')
response.on('data', function (chunk) {
  data.push(chunk);
});
response.on('end', function () {
  resolve(data.join(''));
});
```

