]) {
  console.log(mode, JSON.stringify(vm.runInNewContext(code, context)));
}
```



### How often does it reproduce? Is there a required condition?

The example produced the same output in five runs on each tested version. The getter is defined inside the VM context, directly on `globalThis`.


### What is the expected behavior? Why is that the expected behavior?

Reading the property should propagate the error thrown by its getter to the surrounding `catch`. The result should contain `caught: true` and `message: "getter failure"`, rather than treating the read as successful and returning `undefined`.

This is what happens with `vm.constants.DONT_CONTEXTIFY` on all three tested versions. The default context also catches the exception on v26.2.0.


### What do you see instead?


On v26.9.0 and v26.11.1, the behavior output is:

```text
contextified {"caught":false,"value":"undefined","getterCalls":1}
ordinary {"caught":true,"message":"getter failure","getterCalls":1}
```

The script uses `String()` to make an `undefined` result visible in the JSON output.

| Node | V8 | Default context catches exception | Getter calls in default context |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| v26.2.0 | 14.6.202.34-node.20 | Yes | 3 |
| v26.9.0 | 14.6.202.34-node.32 | No | 1 |
| v26.11.1 | 14.6.202.34-node.37 | No | 1 |

With `DONT_CONTEXTIFY`, the exception is caught and the getter runs once on all three versions.



### Additional information


[#63549](https://github.com/nodejs/node/pull/63549) may be related, but no bisect has been performed and the first affected release has not been identified. This report concerns explicit getter reads, rather than the Proxy property-query issue addressed by [#63742](https://github.com/nodejs/node/pull/63742).
