### Version

v26.10.0 and v26.11.1.

### Platform

Linux arm64.

### Subsystem

lib (abortcontroller)

### What steps will reproduce the bug?

Run `node --expose-gc repro.mjs`:

```js
// node --expose-gc e2.mjs
const sleep = ms => new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));
for (const combine of [false, true]) {
  const refs = [];
  let fired = Array.from({ length: 200 }, () => AbortSignal.timeout(1));
  for (const signal of fired) refs.push(new WeakRef(signal));
  await sleep(50);
  if (combine) for (const signal of fired) AbortSignal.any([signal]);
  fired = undefined;
  for (let round = 0; round < 3; round++) { await sleep(10); globalThis.gc(); }
  console.log(`combine=${combine}: ${refs.filter(ref => ref.deref() !== undefined).length}/200 fired timeouts alive`);
}
```

### How often does it reproduce? Is there a required condition?

Always. The timeout must have fired before it is passed to `AbortSignal.any()`.

### What is the expected behavior? Why is that the expected behavior?

`combine=true: 1/200 fired timeouts alive`, the same as `combine=false`. A composite of a signal that has already aborted is aborted at once, and nothing is left to wait for, so nothing should keep the timeout alive.

### What do you see instead?

`combine=true: 200/200 fired timeouts alive`: every fired timeout passed to `AbortSignal.any()` stays reachable after collection.

### Additional information

It looks as if the timeout is added to `gcPersistentSignals` (#57867) and never removed, since it will never fire again. Passing the same fired timeout to `new Request(url, { signal })` doesn't keep it alive.

