body_text:
### Version

v26.11.1 (also v22.21.1)

### Platform

```text
Darwin 25.5.0 arm64. Also seen on Linux in production.
```

### Subsystem

https

### What steps will reproduce the bug?

```node
// HTTPS_PROXY=https://localhost:8443 NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1 node repro.mjs
import https from 'node:https';
import { createServer } from 'node:net';

// A fake proxy that reads the TLS ClientHello, which carries SNI as plain text
async function check(name, request) {
  const proxy = createServer((socket) => {
    socket.once('data', (clientHello) => {
      console.log(`${name}: proxy received SNI:`, clientHello.includes('localhost'));
      socket.destroy();
    });
  });
  await new Promise((resolve) => proxy.listen(8443, 'localhost', resolve));
  await request().catch(() => {});
  await new Promise((resolve) => proxy.close(resolve));
}

await check('https.get()', () => new Promise((_, reject) => https.get('https://example.com/').on('error', reject)));
await check('fetch()', () => fetch('https://example.com/'));
```
```node
$ HTTPS_PROXY=https://localhost:8443 NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1 node repro.mjs
```
The same happens with `new https.Agent({ proxyEnv: { HTTPS_PROXY: 'https://…' } })`.

### How often does it reproduce? Is there a required condition?

Always, when the proxy URL uses `https://` and its host is a name rather than an IP address.

### What is the expected behavior? Why is that the expected behavior?

```node
https.get(): proxy received SNI: true
fetch(): proxy received SNI: true
```

The TLS connection to an `https://` proxy should send the proxy's host name as SNI, just as `https.request()` does for any host and as `fetch() ` does with the same proxy settings (undici fixed the same problem in [nodejs/undici#2939](https://github.com/nodejs/undici/issues/2939)). Servers that pick their certificate by SNI otherwise answer with a default certificate.

### What do you see instead?

```node
https.get(): proxy received SNI: false
fetch(): proxy received SNI: true
```

Proxies that pick their certificate by SNI then answer with their default certificate. When that certificate doesn't match the proxy's host name or has expired, every request fails, for example with `CERT_HAS_EXPIRED`, while` fetch()` works with the same proxy. Node still verifies the proxy's certificate against its host name, so security isn't affected, only the connection.

### Additional information

ProxyConfig builds the options for the proxy connection from only `host` and `port` ([lib/internal/http.js#L145-L151](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/87db28b6bac0bb3e2919a7843c02ff798a31cd1f/lib/internal/http.js#L145-L151)), and the https agent's `createConnection()` passes them to `tls.connect()` unchanged ([lib/https.js#L399-L401](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/87db28b6bac0bb3e2919a7843c02ff798a31cd1f/lib/https.js#L399-L401), [#L438](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/87db28b6bac0bb3e2919a7843c02ff798a31cd1f/lib/https.js#L438)). Unlike `https.request()`, `tls.connect()` sends no SNI unless `servername` is set. A possible fix is to set `servername` for `https:` proxies whose host isn't an IP address, as undici does.

