body_text:
## This would solve...

When an HTTP/2 server sends GOAWAY, it can say that a request stream was not processed. Undici can retry some requests in this case. With Fetch, a string body becomes a one-use stream. The retry code cannot send it again, so the request times out.

The example sends `foo=bar` on stream 1. GOAWAY says that stream 1 was not processed. On current `main`, Fetch times out and does not open a second stream.

## Reproduction

Run this from the Undici repo root after installing the development dependencies:

```js
const { constants, createSecureServer } = require('node:http2')
const { once } = require('node:events')
const pem = require('@metcoder95/https-pem')
const { Client, fetch } = require('./')

async function main () {
  const server = createSecureServer(await pem.generate({ opts: { keySize: 2048 } }))
  let streams = 0
  let received = ''

  server.on('session', session => session.on('error', () => {}))
  server.on('stream', (stream) => {
    stream.on('error', () => {})

    if (++streams === 1) {
      stream.session.goaway(constants.NGHTTP2_NO_ERROR, 0)
      stream.resume()
      return
    }

    stream.setEncoding('utf8')
    stream.on('data', chunk => { received += chunk })
    stream.on('end', () => {
      stream.respond({ ':status': 200 })
      stream.end('ok')
    })
  })

  server.listen(0)
  await once(server, 'listening')

  const url = `https://localhost:${server.address().port}/`
  const client = new Client(url, {
    allowH2: true,
    connect: { rejectUnauthorized: false }
  })

  try {
    const response = await fetch(url, {
      method: 'POST',
      body: 'foo=bar',
      dispatcher: client,
      signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000)
    })
    console.log(response.status, { streams, received })
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(error.name, { streams, received })
  } finally {
    await client.destroy()
    await new Promise(resolve => server.close(resolve))
  }
}

main()
```

On current `main`, this prints `TimeoutError` with `streams: 1` and an empty `received` value. I expect it to print status `200` with `streams: 2` and `received: 'foo=bar'`.

## The implementation should look like...

Fetch could create a new stream for bodies that always produce the same bytes, such as strings, byte arrays, and Blobs. `ReadableStream` and async iterable bodies should stay one-use.

## I have also considered...

Recreating `FormData` may create a new multipart boundary. The body could then disagree with the existing `Content-Type`. Should `FormData` stay non-replayable unless Undici keeps the original body and boundary? Should this also work through `RetryAgent`?

## Additional context

This came from [PR #5909](https://github.com/nodejs/undici/pull/5909), which was closed with a request to open an issue first. [Issue #3433](https://github.com/nodejs/undici/issues/3433) is about a later request after GOAWAY. This issue is about retrying the request GOAWAY says was not processed.

