body_text:
The dns interceptor is meant to pass requests whose origin is already an IP address straight through. That works for IPv4 literals, but for an IPv6 literal such as `http://[::1]:3000` the interceptor performs a lookup for the string `[::1]`, which fails with `getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND [::1]`. The same request without the interceptor succeeds.

### What should happen

A request to an IPv6 literal origin should be dispatched as is, without a lookup, in the same way an IPv4 literal is.

### Reproduction

```js
const { Agent, interceptors, request } = require('undici')
const http = require('node:http')

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => res.end('ok')).listen(0, '::1', async () => {
  const { port } = server.address()
  const agent = new Agent().compose(interceptors.dns())
  try {
    const res = await request(`http://[::1]:${port}/`, { dispatcher: agent })
    console.log(res.statusCode, await res.body.text())
  } catch (err) {
    console.log(err.code, err.message)
  }
  server.close()
  agent.close()
})
```

Output on main (e8f3423):

```
ENOTFOUND getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND [::1]
```

Expected: `200 ok`.

With a custom `lookup` option the callback is invoked with `origin.hostname === '[::1]'`, so any user lookup has to special case brackets too.

### Root cause

In `lib/interceptor/dns.js` the bypass check is `isIP(origin.hostname) !== 0`. `URL#hostname` keeps the brackets around IPv6 addresses (`new URL('http://[::1]').hostname === '[::1]'`), and `net.isIP('[::1]')` returns 0, so the request falls through to `runLookup()`.

